Intent Rule Check (IRC)
Intent Rule Check is a proposed framework for capturing design intent at the schematic level and assessing whether that intent is preserved throughout physical implementation.
The objective is to identify implementation risks involving device matching, RF-sensitive routing, high-current paths, noise-sensitive signals, clock distribution, and ESD structures before formal design reviews and signoff.
IRC aims to complement conventional physical verification by connecting layout decisions to their electrical purpose and highlighting risks that geometric and connectivity checks alone may not capture.

Future Direction
Beyond intent checking, IRC could support a knowledge base connecting design requirements, floorplan decisions, layout practices, and validated silicon outcomes. Over time, this knowledge could inform placement, isolation, and routing recommendations for future designs.

Status
Independent research concept under development. Implementation methods and validation results will be documented as the work progresses.
